insert into public.problems (id, title, source, url, difficulty_rating, statement_md, input_format_md, output_format_md, constraints_text, sample_io, target_complexity) values ('sbc-2013-final-d-disjoint-water-supply', 'Disjoint water supply', 'SBC Maratona', 'https://maratona.sbc.org.br/hist/2013/resultados13/data/contest_onesided.pdf', 1700, 'Problem D
                                  Disjoint water supply
Nlogonia is a queendom that consists of several cities located on a big mountain. The capital city
is Logville, located on the mountain peak. Logville has a huge lake with a perfectly round shape,
appropriately named “The Big O”. This is the only lake with drinkable water in the entire queendom,
so it is used to supply all cities. Some cities in Nlogonia are connected with water pipes that allow
the distribution of the water. As there are no pumps, each pipe carries water from a city to another
city at a lower altitude, using gravity.
    Nlogonia’s water system has been a source of worries for the Queen, because since cities depend
on other cities for their water supply, hot discussions occur about how much water a city is allowed
to use. A water supply path is a sequence of cities in decreasing order of altitude, starting in Logville
and such that there is a pipe connecting each pair of consecutive cities in the sequence. Two cities
have disjoint water supply if and only if there exist two water supply paths, one supply path ending in
each of the cities, such that Logville is the only city that is present in both paths. Notice that Logville
itself has disjoint water supply with every other city.
    The Queen considers disjoint water supply a nice property because it reduces dependency problems
and also avoids water outages to spread as quickly through Nlogonia. She therefore ordered a survey
to assess the current state of water supply disjointness in the whole queendom. Being the cleverest
advisors in the Queen’s court, you have been summoned to help calculate the number of pairs of
distinct cities that have disjoint water supply.
Input
    The first line contains two integers C (2 ≤ C ≤ 1000) and P (1 ≤ P ≤ 105 ), representing
respectively the number of cities and the number of water pipes in Nlogonia. Cities are identified with
different integers from 1 to C, in strictly decreasing order of altitude (no two cities have the same
altitude); Logville is city 1. Each of the next P lines describes a pipe with two integers U and V
(1 ≤ U < V ≤ C) indicating that the pipe connects city U with city V . You may assume that no two
pipes connect the same pair of cities, and that for each city in Nlogonia there is at least one water
supply path that ends in it.
Output
   Output a line with an integer representing the number of pairs of distinct cities that have disjoint
water supply.
 Sample input 1                                       Sample output 1

 6 6                                                  14
 1 2
 1 3
 1 4
 2 5
 2 6
 3 6

ICPC 

 Sample input 2                       Sample output 2

 8 11                                 26
 1 2
 1 3
 1 4
 2 5
 3 4
 6 7
 3 6
 3 7
 4 8
 2 6
 5 6

ICPC', 'The first line contains two integers C (2 ≤ C ≤ 1000) and P (1 ≤ P ≤ 105 ), representing
respectively the number of cities and the number of water pipes in Nlogonia. Cities are identified with
different integers from 1 to C, in strictly decreasing order of altitude (no two cities have the same
altitude); Logville is city 1. Each of the next P lines describes a pipe with two integers U and V
(1 ≤ U < V ≤ C) indicating that the pipe connects city U with city V . You may assume that no two
pipes connect the same pair of cities, and that for each city in Nlogonia there is at least one water
supply path that ends in it.', 'Output a line with an integer representing the number of pairs of distinct cities that have disjoint
water supply.', '2 ≤ C ≤ 1000) and P (1 ≤ P ≤ 105 ), representing; 1 ≤ U < V ≤ C) indicating that the pipe connects city ', '[]'::jsonb, 'O(N log N)');
