insert into public.problems (id, title, source, url, difficulty_rating, statement_md, input_format_md, output_format_md, constraints_text, sample_io, target_complexity) values ('sbc-2024-final-l-latin-squares', 'Latin Squares', 'SBC Maratona', 'https://scorelatam.naquadah.com.br/latam-2024/contest.pdf', 2500, 'Problem L – Latin Squares
                                      Author : Arthur Nascimento, Brasil

A Latin square of size N is an N × N matrix where each entry is an integer between 1 and N ,
and no two entries in the same row or column are equal.
Alice has a Latin square of size N , and she applied a list of transformations to it. Each
transformation swaps either two distinct rows or two distinct columns of the matrix. The
transformations are applied sequentially: the first transformation is applied to the original
matrix, the second to the result of the first transformation, and so on. The final result is, of
course, an N × N matrix.
Alice will not reveal any matrix to you, only the list of transformations. Your task is to
determine whether there exists an original matrix that, after applying all transformations, is
identical to the final matrix. If such a matrix exists, you must also provide a possible content
for this original matrix.

Input
The first line contains two integers N (2 ≤ N ≤ 500) and T (1 ≤ T ≤ 105 ), indicating
respectively the size of the Latin square and the number of transformations.
The next T lines describe the transformations, in the order they are applied, one transformation
per line. Each of these lines contains a character X and two integers I and J (1 ≤ I, J ≤ N
and I 6= J). The character X is either the uppercase letter “R” or the uppercase letter “C”
indicating respectively that rows I and J, or columns I and J are swapped.

Output
If there exists an original matrix that, after applying all transformations, is identical to the
final matrix, output N lines with N integers each, representing any such matrix.
If no such matrix exists, output a line with the character “*” (asterisk) instead.

 Sample input 1                                          Sample output 1

 4 4                                                     1 2 3 4
 R 1 2                                                   2 1 4 3
 C 2 1                                                   3 4 1 2
 R 3 4                                                   4 3 2 1
 C 3 4

 Sample input 2                                          Sample output 2

 4 3                                                     *
 R 1 2
 R 1 2
 R 2 1

ICPC', 'The first line contains two integers N (2 ≤ N ≤ 500) and T (1 ≤ T ≤ 105 ), indicating
respectively the size of the Latin square and the number of transformations.
The next T lines describe the transformations, in the order they are applied, one transformation
per line. Each of these lines contains a character X and two integers I and J (1 ≤ I, J ≤ N
and I 6= J). The character X is either the uppercase letter “R” or the uppercase letter “C”
indicating respectively that rows I and J, or columns I and J are swapped.', 'If there exists an original matrix that, after applying all transformations, is identical to the
final matrix, output N lines with N integers each, representing any such matrix.
If no such matrix exists, output a line with the character “*” (asterisk) instead.', '2 ≤ N ≤ 500) and T (1 ≤ T ≤ 105 ), indicating; 1 ≤ I, J ≤ N', '[]'::jsonb, 'O(N log N)');
insert into public.solution_approaches (id, problem_id, approach_name, paradigms, algorithms, data_structures, key_insights, editorial_summary_md) values ('sbc-2024-final-l-latin-squares-a1', 'sbc-2024-final-l-latin-squares', 'SBC 2024 — Final (LATAM) — standard', ARRAY['Ad-Hoc']::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY[]::text[], ARRAY['SBC 2024 — Final (LATAM), problem L. Auto-tagged from statement; refine after solving.']::text[], 'From SBC 2024 — Final (LATAM). Review tags and write a proper editorial after solving.');
insert into public.problems (id, title, source, url, difficulty_rating, statement_md, input_format_md, output_format_md, constraints_text, sample_io, target_complexity) values ('sbc-2025-final-l-lonely-creatures', 'Lonely Creatures', 'SBC Maratona', 'https://scorelatam.naquadah.com.br/latam-2025/contest.pdf', 2500, 'Problem L – Lonely Creatures
                               Author : Paulo Cezar Pereira Costa, Brasil

There are N creatures, numbered from 1 to N , that live in an infinite city represented by the 2D plane.
Creature i lives on the straight line y = Mi · x + Ci ; it may move anywhere along that line. No two
creatures share the same line, but a meeting occurs if their lines intersect. In that case, the intersection
point is called a meeting point of the two creatures.
The city has just built a new playground. The playground’s boundary is the (axis-aligned) parabola
y = A · x2 + B, where A > 0. The playground is the open region y > A · x2 + B, so points exactly on
the boundary are not inside the playground.
Now the city officials want to know whether the new playground is reducing loneliness, and that’s where
your help is needed. Your task is to count how many distinct pairs of creatures have a meeting point
that lies strictly inside the playground. Each pair is counted once; the order of the creatures in a pair
does not matter.

Input
The first line contains three integers N (2 ≤ N ≤ 105 ), A (1 ≤ A ≤ 104 ) and B (−104 ≤ B ≤ 104 ),
indicating that there are N creatures and that the playground is the open region of the 2D plane
y > A · x2 + B. Each creature is identified by a distinct integer from 1 to N .
The i-th of the next N lines contains two integers Mi and Ci (−104 ≤ Mi , Ci ≤ 104 ), representing that
creature i lives on the straight line y = Mi · x + Ci . It is guaranteed that no two lines are identical.

Output
Output a single line with an integer indicating the number of distinct pairs of creatures whose meeting
point lies strictly inside the playground.

 Sample input 1                                        Sample output 1
 3 1 0                                                 0
 -1 -1
 2 4
 2 0

Explanation of sample 1 :

Creature 1 meets creatures 2 and 3, but in both
cases the meeting point lies outside the play-
ground. There are no other meeting points, so
the answer is 0.

ICPC 

 Sample input 2                                     Sample output 2
 4 1 -2                                             5
 -1 0
 0 1
 1 0
 2 1

Explanation of sample 2 :

Each pair of creatures have a meeting point. How-
ever, the meeting point of creatures 3 and 4 lies
on the playground boundary (y = A · x2 + B), so
that pair is excluded from the count.

 Sample input 3                                     Sample output 3
 4 1 0                                              6
 7 -3
 5 -2
 3 -1
 1 0

ICPC', 'The first line contains three integers N (2 ≤ N ≤ 105 ), A (1 ≤ A ≤ 104 ) and B (−104 ≤ B ≤ 104 ),
indicating that there are N creatures and that the playground is the open region of the 2D plane
y > A · x2 + B. Each creature is identified by a distinct integer from 1 to N .
The i-th of the next N lines contains two integers Mi and Ci (−104 ≤ Mi , Ci ≤ 104 ), representing that
creature i lives on the straight line y = Mi · x + Ci . It is guaranteed that no two lines are identical.', 'Output a single line with an integer indicating the number of distinct pairs of creatures whose meeting
point lies strictly inside the playground.', '2 ≤ N ≤ 105 ), A (1 ≤ A ≤ 104 ) and B (−104 ≤ B ≤ 104 ; 104 ≤ Mi , Ci ≤ 104 ), representing that', '[]'::jsonb, 'O(N log N)');
